states.activities:
NHS England will provide the relevant records from the MHSDS datasets to the Inquiry.  The Data will contain no direct identifying data items.  The Data will be pseudonymised and individuals cannot be reidentified through linkage with other data in the possession of the Recipient.

The Data within the NHS England Secure Data Environment (SDE) will be unfiltered however further processing will be undertaken once access has been granted to the SDE. NHS England subject matter experts will support the Expert Health Statisticians to write a script that will minimise the Data as per the scope of the Inquiry. As such, data will be minimised to NHS-funded mental health inpatient service providers in Essex, and may also be minimised to include Essex residents receiving care out of area.

The Data will not be transferred to any other location.

The Data will be stored on servers at NHS England.

The Data will be accessed by authorised personnel via remote access.  

The Controller(s) must confirm and provide evidence upon audit by NHS England that access via any remote device complies with the data security obligations within this DSA and the Data Sharing Framework Contract.

For remote access:
•	Remote access will only be from secure locations situated within the territory of use (as further restricted elsewhere within the DSA if so done) stated within this DSA; 
•	Access controls granting users the minimum level of access required are in place;
•	Remote access is only via secure connections (e.g., VPNs or secure protocols) to protect data;
•	Multifactor authentication (MFA) is required for remote access;
•	Device security, including up-to-date software and operating systems, antivirus software, and enabled firewalls are utilised for the remote access;
•	All remote access is undertaken within the scope of the organisation’s DSPT (or other security arrangements as per this DSA) and complies with the organisation’s remote access policy. 

The above applies in addition to any condition set out elsewhere within the DSA (e.g. who may carry out processing, and for what purpose).

Oxford University Innovation (OUI) are the relevant institutional data processor for the Expert Health Statistician. OUI is a subsidiary of the University of Oxford and are the contracting entity the Inquiry has engaged for the Chief Investigator’s services.

The aggregated information derived from the Data will provide denominator data for the anonymised data from the Inquiry’s List of Deceased, which provides a record of all deaths known to the Inquiry as falling within scope of its Terms of Reference. As the data provided in the MHSDS is pseudonymised and the List of Deceased data is anonymised to the Inquiry’s Expert Health Statisticians, the extent of linkage will be limited to ward- and hospital-level analyses.
